  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-2x$ and $f(2)=5.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ to estimate the value of $f(2.4).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f(2.4)≈$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(2)=5,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(2)=3(2)^{2}-2(2)=12-4=8.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=5+8(x-2).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(2.4)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=2.4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.4) & ≈L(2.4) \\\\  & =5+8(2.4-2) \\\\  & =5+8(0.4) \\\\  & =5+3.2 \\\\  & =8.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
